---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

// Cornerstone pillar #1 — "What custom software actually costs in 2026".
// AEO-first: 150-word answer block up top, one H2 per real PAA question,
// FAQPage + Article + BreadcrumbList schema mirroring the visible copy.
// All figures match the published Services FAQ and home-page metrics — no
// number appears here that isn't already stated elsewhere on the site.

const datePublished = '2026-07-04';
const dateModified = '2026-07-04';

// The 150-word answer block (lifted verbatim by AI Overviews / assistants).
const answer = `Most custom software in 2026 costs between $25,000 and $250,000, and the figure is set by scope, not by hours or page count. A focused internal tool or marketing platform with real workflows starts around $25,000–$50,000. A full ecommerce or multi-role platform with integrations typically runs $50,000–$150,000. Enterprise-grade systems with AI, complex data, and security requirements reach $150,000–$250,000 and beyond. The biggest cost drivers are the number of user roles, the count of third-party integrations, and how much of your operation the software has to run. Below roughly $25,000, a custom build is usually the wrong tool. An off-the-shelf product will serve you better, and an honest partner will say so. We scope and price one phase at a time, so you fund the riskiest part first and see working software before committing the full budget.`;

const tiers = [
  {
    range: '$25K – $50K',
    label: 'Focused tool or platform',
    body: 'A single-purpose internal tool or a marketing platform with real workflows: accounts, an admin dashboard, intake that routes itself. One or two integrations. The first usable version typically ships in 4–8 weeks.',
  },
  {
    range: '$50K – $150K',
    label: 'Multi-role platform',
    body: 'A full ecommerce or operations platform: several user roles, an admin back end, and integrations to your CRM, payment processor, and reporting. This is where most mid-market builds land.',
  },
  {
    range: '$150K – $250K+',
    label: 'Enterprise-grade system',
    body: 'Complex data models, embedded AI, migrations off legacy systems, and security requirements an enterprise team will review. Priced and delivered in phases, never as one monolithic bid.',
  },
];

// FAQ — each item is a real "People also ask" question, answered independently
// in 100–150 words. This array drives BOTH the visible FAQ and the FAQPage
// schema, so the two mirror each other (required for legitimacy).
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What actually drives the cost of a custom software project?',
    a: 'Scope drives cost, and scope breaks down into a few concrete levers: the number of distinct user roles, the count of third-party systems it has to integrate with, how much of your operation the software is expected to run, and whether it handles regulated or sensitive data. A read-only dashboard for one team is cheap; a platform that gates access by role, syncs with your CRM and payment processor, and routes work automatically is not. AI features and data migrations off legacy systems add cost because they add risk, not because the code is exotic. What does not meaningfully move the number is page count or visual polish; those are the cheap part. If someone quotes you by the page, they are pricing the wrong thing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Custom software vs. a template, a freelancer, or a big agency: what is the real cost difference?',
    a: 'A template site costs hundreds to low thousands and is the right call when all you need is pages. A freelancer is cheaper per hour but you carry the integration risk and the bus-factor risk when they move on. A large agency will quote $250,000 and up for the same platform a focused senior team ships for $50,000–$150,000, because you are funding account managers and overhead layers between you and whoever writes the code. The honest comparison is not price-per-hour, it is total cost to a working system that you own and can change. A template that you outgrow in a year and have to replace is the most expensive option of all. Match the tool to whether the software has to do work or just display information.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a custom build priced as a fixed bid or by phase?',
    a: 'We price by phase, and so should anyone building custom software honestly. Fixed-bid-the-whole-thing pricing forces everyone to pretend the specification is perfect on day one, and custom software never works that way. The spec is wrong until real users touch working software. Instead we scope and quote one phase at a time: a defined deliverable, a clear price, and something running at the end of it. You decide whether to fund the next phase based on what you have seen, not on a guess made before anything existed. This keeps you in control of spend and means the budget follows value. A vendor who insists on one large fixed bid is transferring their estimation risk onto you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does a custom software project take?',
    a: 'A focused first release typically ships in 6 to 12 weeks. The work runs in phases rather than one long build: discovery and scope take 1–2 weeks, the first usable version lands in 4–8 weeks, and it iterates from there based on real usage. Timeline is driven by decision speed and integration complexity far more than by raw engineering hours. Projects stall when feedback is slow or a third-party system is undocumented, not because the code is hard. The first phase is sized deliberately small so you have something to react to early, instead of waiting months only to discover the spec was wrong. Longer timelines cost more mostly because they signal larger scope, not because time itself is the meter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know a custom build is worth it for my business?',
    a: 'Custom software earns its cost when it removes work that people are currently doing by hand, or captures revenue that manual processes are leaking. The signal is concrete: a team copy-pasting data between four apps, a spreadsheet that has quietly become load-bearing, intake that a person triages by reading every email, or orders lost because two systems do not talk. Recent builds cut email triage time by about 70%, sped deal evaluation by 50%, and lifted task completion by 65%; those numbers, not the software itself, are the return. If you cannot name the manual hours or the leaked revenue a build would recover, it is too early to spend $25,000 on custom software, and we will tell you so.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should I NOT build custom software?',
    a: 'When an off-the-shelf product already does the job, do not build. Below roughly $25,000 of value, a custom build rarely pays for itself against a good SaaS subscription or a well-chosen template. Skip custom when your process is still changing weekly and you have not yet found the workflow worth cementing: you would be paying to harden a guess. Skip it when the "requirement" is really one report you could pull by hand once a quarter. A custom platform is worth it precisely when your operation is stable enough to know what it does, complex enough that no product fits it cleanly, and busy enough that the manual workaround is costing real hours. Anything short of that, and the honest answer is: not yet.',
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "What custom software actually costs in 2026",
  "description": "Real price ranges, what drives the number up or down, and how custom software is scoped and priced. The pricing guide no agency publishes.",
  "datePublished": datePublished,
  "dateModified": dateModified,
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Jay Smith" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://10xstudio.dev/#org" },
  "mainEntityOfPage": "https://10xstudio.dev/pricing/",
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://10xstudio.dev/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Pricing", "item": "https://10xstudio.dev/pricing/" },
  ],
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a },
  })),
};
---
<Base
  title="What Custom Software Actually Costs in 2026 - 10x Studio"
  description="Most custom software costs $25K–$250K, set by scope not hours. Real price ranges, what drives the number, and how a custom build is scoped and priced by phase."
  active="pricing"
  ogType="article"
  datePublished={datePublished}
  dateModified={dateModified}
  jsonLd={[articleSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema]}
>

  <!-- HERO -->
  <div class="grid-bg">
    <section class="mk-wrap pricing-hero">
      <h1 class="mk-h1">What custom software<br>actually <span class="accent">costs.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">No agency in this market publishes prices. We do. Here are the real ranges, what moves the number up or down, and how a build is scoped so you fund value instead of a guess.</p>
      <div class="titleblock">
        <div>Guide<b>What custom software costs</b></div>
        <div>Studio<b>10x Studio</b></div>
        <div>Updated<b>July 2026</b></div>
        <div>Author<b>Jay Smith</b></div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="mk-wrap">

    <!-- PART 01: Short answer block -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">01</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <h2 class="mk-h2">The short answer.</h2>
        <div class="answer-block">
          <p>{answer}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 02: Price tiers -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">02</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <h2 class="mk-h2">Three price ranges, set by scope.</h2>
        <table class="spec pricing-tiers">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Range</th>
              <th scope="col">Type</th>
              <th scope="col">What it covers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {tiers.map((t) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{t.range}</td>
                <td>{t.label}</td>
                <td>{t.body}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="pricing-note">These are the same ranges we quote in a first conversation, not teaser pricing. The number inside a range is set by user roles, integrations, and how much of your operation the software has to run.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 03: FAQ -->
    <section class="part">
      <span class="part-no">FAQ</span>
      <div class="part-body">
        <h2 class="mk-h2">The questions behind the number.</h2>
        <div class="pricing-faq-list">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <details class="pricing-faq-item">
              <summary><h3 class="pricing-faq-q">{f.q}</h3><span class="pricing-faq-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
              <p class="pricing-faq-a">{f.a}</p>
            </details>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="cta-band-inner">
      <h2>Want a real number for <span class="accent">your build?</span></h2>
      <a href="/contact/" class="mk-btn mk-btn--yellow mk-btn--lg">
        Start a project conversation <Icon name="arrow-right" size={18} />
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>

<style>
  /* Hero */
  .pricing-hero { padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 110px) 0; }
  .pricing-hero .lede { margin-top: 24px; }
  .pricing-hero .titleblock { margin-top: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px); }

  /* Answer block */
  .answer-block {
    margin-top: 32px;
    border: var(--line);
    background: var(--sheet);
    padding: 28px 32px;
    max-width: 76ch;
  }
  .answer-block p {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--graphite-soft);
  }

  /* Pricing tiers table */
  .pricing-tiers { margin-top: 40px; }
  .pricing-tiers td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    width: 18%;
  }
  .pricing-tiers td:nth-child(2) {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--type-title);
    color: var(--graphite);
    width: 26%;
  }
  .pricing-note {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--graphite-soft);
    max-width: 70ch;
  }

  /* FAQ */
  .pricing-faq-list { margin-top: 40px; }
  .pricing-faq-item { border-top: var(--line); }
  .pricing-faq-item:last-of-type { border-bottom: var(--line); }
  .pricing-faq-item summary {
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 20px;
    padding: 20px 0;
    transition: background-color var(--dur);
  }
  .pricing-faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .pricing-faq-item[open] summary { background: var(--bone-deep); padding-inline: 16px; margin-inline: -16px; }
  .pricing-faq-q {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--type-title);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--graphite);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .pricing-faq-a {
    padding: 0 16px 20px;
    max-width: 70ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--graphite-soft);
  }

  .pricing-faq-mark {
    flex: none;
    position: relative;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
  }
  .pricing-faq-mark::before,
  .pricing-faq-mark::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    background: var(--graphite);
    transition: opacity var(--dur), transform var(--dur);
  }
  .pricing-faq-mark::before { top: 8px; left: 0; width: 18px; height: 1px; }
  .pricing-faq-mark::after  { top: 0; left: 8px; width: 1px; height: 18px; }
  .pricing-faq-item[open] .pricing-faq-mark::after { opacity: 0; transform: rotate(90deg); }

  @media (max-width: 980px) {
    .pricing-faq-item[open] summary { padding-inline: 0; margin-inline: 0; }
    .pricing-faq-a { padding-inline: 0; }
    .pricing-tiers td:first-child,
    .pricing-tiers td:nth-child(2) { width: auto; }
    .answer-block { padding: 20px; }
  }
</style>
